# neter (`nr`)

<!-- GENERATED by `nr skills`. Do NOT edit: the next `nr skills` run overwrites this file. -->

`nr` is the helper CLI for this **neter** application. Run it from the project
root unless a command says otherwise; `nr skills` can itself be run from a
subdirectory and finds the nearest `go.mod`.

- Detected persistence stack: **{{.Kind}}**{{if .IsSQLC}} (PostgreSQL + pgx + sqlc){{end}}{{if .IsEnt}} (legacy Ent + MySQL){{end}}
{{if .IsUnknown}}- The stack could not be detected. Before generating CRUD, inspect the repository: `sqlc.yaml` or `db/query/` means sqlc; `internal/data/ent/schema/` means Ent. Then use the matching recipe below.{{end}}

## Choose the command by intent

| When you need to… | Use this | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Work on backend and frontend interactively | `nr dev` | Starts the backend development command and the frontend dev server. Use when changing either side and hot reload is wanted. |
| Run only the backend once | `nr run` | Builds and starts the app; pass app arguments with `-c`, e.g. `nr run -c "--config ./config.yml"`. |
| Build a local binary | `nr build` | Builds for the current OS and architecture. |
| Produce a release binary with the embedded SPA | `nr build --prod` | `--prod` implies `--trim --web`; use `--linux --arch arm64` when cross-compiling. |
| Build or run with freshly built SPA assets, but not release defaults | `nr build --web` / `nr run --web` | Builds `web/` first and copies its output into embedded assets. |
| Add a business capability | `nr gen biz -n <name> ...` | Start with this for biz/repository/DI scaffolding; choose the flags in [Biz scaffolding](#biz-scaffolding). |
| Add a HTTP route scaffold | `nr gen route -n <name>` | Add `--pkg v1` for a route subpackage; use `--v2` only when the project uses the v2 route template. |
| Inspect routes or create frontend API contracts | `nr route-info …` | Use it for Gin route discovery; see [Routes and TypeScript](#routes-and-typescript). |
| Add a database schema change | `nr migrate new <name>` | Create paired up/down files, edit **both**, review, then apply with `nr migrate up`. |
| Regenerate generated dependencies | `nr wire`, `nr gen mock`, `make sqlc` | Pick the generator that owns the modified source; see [After changing code](#after-changing-code). |
| See all flags or an unfamiliar command | `nr <command> --help` | Prefer help over guessing flags or generated file locations. |

## Development and build workflow

- Use `nr dev` for normal full-stack development. Defaults are backend `nr run -dr`
and frontend `pnpm run dev` in `web/`; change unusual layouts with
`--backend-cmd`, `--backend-dir`, `--frontend-dir`, `--pm`, or `--frontend-cmd`.
- Use `nr run` when only the backend is relevant or a one-off executable is
needed. `--wire` regenerates Wire before building; `--delete` removes the
built executable after it exits. Do not use `--rm` unless deleting `build/` is
intentional.
- Use `nr build` for a binary, `nr build --web` when the binary must include
current frontend assets, and `nr build --prod` for production-oriented builds.
`--output <file>` is only appropriate when building one target. Use
`--deploy` only for a Linux-only build and only when deployment settings in
`neter.yml` are configured.
- `nr config-example` prints all supported `neter.yml` settings. Use it when
adding or checking build, frontend, or deployment configuration—not as a
replacement for the project's existing config.

## Biz scaffolding

Use `nr gen biz -n <name>` to create a new biz unit. The name is converted to
snake case for filenames (for example, `-n userProfile` creates
`internal/biz/user_profile_biz.go`). Pick only the optional pieces required:

| Need | Flag | Result / follow-up |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Plain biz + repository skeleton | *(no extra flags)* | Default choice for a custom use case. Implement business and data methods yourself. |
| An interface that can be mocked in tests | `--with-iface` | Adds a biz interface and mockgen directive. Run `nr gen mock -n <name>` after changing its interface. |
| Request/response parameter structs | `--with-params` | Adds `internal/domain/params/<name>_params.go`; use for transport input/output types. |
| HTTP-independent command/query contracts | `--with-contracts` | Adds Command/Query types; use where handlers should not leak HTTP types into biz code. |
| No repository at all | `--no-repo` | Use for orchestration or external-service-only biz logic. Do not combine it when the capability needs a data repository. |
| CRUD-shaped database access | `--with-crud` plus the stack-specific model flag | Generates only a starting point. Add schema/queries and implement the generated placeholders. |
| Avoid automatic DI regeneration | `--skip-wire` | Advanced: use only if you will run `nr wire`/`wire` yourself after reviewing providers. |

After adding or changing providers, regenerate Wire from the package that owns
them (`cd cmd/app && wire`, `cd internal/cmd_app && wire`, or `nr wire` to
regenerate every `wire.go` below the current directory). Check the diff before
continuing; generated DI code should not be hand-edited.

### Generate a route

```bash
nr gen route -n user
nr gen route -n user -p v1        # route belongs in a subpackage such as v1
nr gen route -n user --skip-wire  # only if Wire will be regenerated manually
```

Use a route scaffold when adding a new Gin handler/registration structure. Do
not use it merely to add an endpoint to an existing route file—edit that route
and handler directly, then regenerate route documentation/contracts if needed.

{{if .IsSQLC}}
## sqlc stack (this project)

**SQL is the source of truth.** Use sqlc for new persisted resources; do not
run `nr new`, `nr gen ent`, or `nr show ent`, which target legacy Ent and refuse
to run in this stack.

### When adding or changing a persisted resource

1. **Schema change:** create a migration if the database schema changes:
   `nr migrate new add_orders`. Edit both generated files under
   `db/migrations/`; the down migration must reverse the up migration.
2. **Query change:** add or update SQL in `db/query/orders.sql`. Follow the
   generated-method naming convention: `ListOrders`, `GetOrder`,
   `CreateOrder`, `UpdateOrder`, `DeleteOrder`.
3. **Generated Go:** run `make sqlc` (or `sqlc generate`) after changing
   migrations/query definitions that affect sqlc. Never hand-edit
   `internal/data/sqlc/**`.
4. **Biz/repository shape:** for a new CRUD resource, scaffold it with:

   ```bash
   nr gen biz -n order --with-crud --model Order --plural Orders
   ```

   `--model` is the sqlc model type; `--plural` controls the list method name
   and defaults to `<Model>s`. Then replace generated
   `errOrderNotImplemented` placeholders with calls to `data.Queries`.
5. **DI and verification:** regenerate Wire if providers changed, then run
   relevant tests (`make test` when available).

Use a migration for durable schema changes, not for a query-only change. Use
`--with-crud` only for conventional list/get/create/update/delete access;
create a plain biz scaffold for domain-specific workflows.

### Database migrations

```bash
nr migrate new add_orders       # create 000NNN_add_orders.{up,down}.sql
# edit and review both files
nr migrate status               # inspect current version before applying/rolling back
nr migrate up                   # apply every pending migration
nr migrate up --steps 1         # apply only the next migration
nr migrate down --steps 1       # roll back the latest migration
nr migrate down --all           # roll back all applied migrations: destructive
nr migrate force <version>      # dirty-state recovery only; does not run SQL
```

Use `force` only after understanding and manually repairing the schema/history;
it changes the recorded version without applying migrations. DSN precedence is
`--dsn` > `NETER_DSN` / `DATABASE_URL` > `config.yml db:`. A deployed binary
can apply its embedded migrations at startup with `db.autoMigrate: true` (or
`NETER_AUTO_MIGRATE=true`); use this only when deployment policy permits it.

`nr route-info` prints a notice for sqlc projects and is not maintained here.
{{end}}

{{if .IsEnt}}
## Legacy Ent stack (this project)

Persistence is Ent + MySQL and the schema lives in `internal/data/ent/schema`.
Use Ent commands for entity/schema changes:

```bash
nr new User                    # create an Ent schema file
# edit internal/data/ent/schema/user.go, then:
nr gen ent                     # run go generate ./ent and refresh Ent code
nr show ent                    # inspect/describe the Ent schema
nr gen biz -n user --with-crud --ent-name User
```

Use `nr new <Name>` only when introducing a new Ent entity. Use `nr gen ent`
after editing an existing Ent schema or adding a new one. `--ent-name` must be
the Ent entity name and is needed with `--with-crud`; use a plain biz scaffold
for non-CRUD behavior.

`nr migrate new/up/down/status/force` only operates on file-based SQL under
`db/migrations`; it does **not** apply Ent schema changes. For Ent schema work,
run `nr gen ent` and then use the application's own Ent migration workflow.
A project switches to sqlc behavior if `sqlc.yaml` or `db/query/` is present.
{{end}}

{{if .IsUnknown}}
## Persistence stack not detected

Do not guess a CRUD model flag. First inspect the project:

- `sqlc.yaml` or `db/query/` present → use the sqlc workflow: SQL/migrations,
  `make sqlc`, then `nr gen biz -n order --with-crud --model Order --plural Orders`.
- `internal/data/ent/schema/` present → use the Ent workflow: schema,
  `nr gen ent`, then `nr gen biz -n order --with-crud --ent-name Order`.
- Neither present → create a plain `nr gen biz -n order` and follow the
  project's existing persistence conventions before adding a new stack.
{{end}}

## Routes and TypeScript

Use these commands only for Gin route inspection/documentation or generated
frontend contracts—not as a substitute for compiling and testing the server.

```bash
nr route-info                                      # terminal curl examples
nr route-info --format md --output docs/routes.md  # write route docs
nr route-info export -o docs/routes.json           # explicit file export
nr route-info -f users -p v1                       # limit by path/handler and route package
nr route-info gen-ts                               # write per-route *.gen.ts files under web/src/api/generated
nr route-info gen-ts --check                       # CI check: fail if contracts are missing/stale
```

Use `--output file.ts` with `gen-ts` only when a single generated TypeScript
file is required; otherwise use a directory for per-route files. Do not edit
`*.gen.ts` by hand—change routes/types and regenerate instead.

## Tests, mocks, and generated output

- Run `make test` for the project's normal test target (or `go test ./...` if
  no Make target exists). Run focused tests while iterating.
- Use `nr gen mock` after changing interfaces in `internal/biz/*_biz.go`; use
  `nr gen mock -n user` for one biz and `--with-test` to add missing test
  stubs. It requires `mockgen` (`go install go.uber.org/mock/mockgen@latest`).
- Use `make mock` where the project provides it. Generated mocks can also be
  refreshed with `go generate ./internal/biz/mocks/...`.
- Do not hand-edit generated code: `internal/data/sqlc/**`, `*_gen.go`,
  `assets/dist/**`, generated mock files, generated `*.gen.ts`, or this
  `SKILL.md`.

## Frontend / SPA

- Source is normally `web/` (Vite). Use `nr dev` for its dev server and
  `nr build --web`/`nr build --prod` when the Go binary must embed it.
- The build copies `web/dist` to `assets/dist`, which is embedded into the Go
  binary. Keep `web/vite.config.ts` `build.manifest: true`; SPA middleware
  reads `.vite/manifest.json`.
- A fresh clone may have no `assets/dist`: backend startup still works but SPA
  routes return 404 until the frontend is built.

## Final checks

Before completing a change: regenerate every affected generator (sqlc, Ent,
Wire, mocks, routes/TypeScript), inspect generated diffs, run focused tests,
and run the project's broader test target when practical. Use
`nr <command> --help` whenever flags, application layout, or deployment
behavior are uncertain.
